# pve-thin-pool — lifted VERBATIM out of prometheusrule.yaml (comments stripped; the manifest is
# their one home) so `promtool test rules` can load them: promtool needs upstream-format rules
# (top-level `groups:`), a PrometheusRule keeps them under `.spec`. Named .promtool-rules rather
# than .yaml on purpose — manifest-lint kubeconforms EVERY *.yaml under argocd/resources.
#
# DRIFT-PINNED by scripts/prometheus-rules-lint.sh (expr+for equality per alert name against the
# CR) — the homelab#337 hazard, a stale hand copy testing the OLD expr and passing vacuously, is
# what that pin closes. Re-copy after every expr edit; one line, run as written:
#
#   devbox run -- yq -o=json '[.spec.groups[] | select(.name == "pve-thin-pool")]' argocd/resources/pve-metrics/prometheusrule.yaml | devbox run -- yq -P '{"groups": .}'
#
# (The node-reboots group in the same CR has its own copy + fixture beside this one.)
groups:
  - name: pve-thin-pool
    rules:
      - alert: PveThinPoolFillingUp
        expr: pve_lvm_thin_pool_data_percent > 80
        for: 10m
        labels:
          severity: warning
          triage: dig
        annotations:
          summary: '{{ $labels.host }} thin pool {{ $labels.vg }}/{{ $labels.lv }} at {{ $value | printf "%.1f" }}%'
          description: 'An LVM thin pool carrying Talos VMs is above 80%. Check the VG''s free extents first (`pve_lvm_vg_free_bytes{host="{{ $labels.host }}",vg="{{ $labels.vg }}"}`): pve''s are ~0, so autoextend cannot act there and the human IS the autoextend. At 100% every VM on the pool pauses on io-error together — on pve that includes the control plane. Return blocks now: trigger the fstrim CronJobs (`kubectl -n node-maintenance create job --from=cronjob/<node> ...`), then read `lvs -o lv_name,data_percent {{ $labels.vg }}` on {{ $labels.host }}. Recipe: docs/runbook.md §"Reclaiming thin-pool space from a Talos VM".'
      - alert: PveThinPoolAlmostFull
        expr: pve_lvm_thin_pool_data_percent > 90
        for: 2m
        labels:
          severity: critical
          triage: now
        annotations:
          summary: '{{ $labels.host }} thin pool {{ $labels.vg }}/{{ $labels.lv }} at {{ $value | printf "%.1f" }}% — VMs pause at 100%'
          description: 'Under 10% headroom on a pool carrying Talos VMs — on pve that pool carries the control plane. One image pull or one Longhorn rebuild finishes it. Act on {{ $labels.host }} directly: fstrim the guests (runbook recipe), or destroy a sacrificial guest (`qm destroy 9001` freed 26 points on 2026-09-03). If VMs are already paused: `qm status <vmid> --verbose | grep qmpstatus`, free space, then `qm resume <vmid>`.'
      - alert: PveThinPoolMetadataHigh
        expr: pve_lvm_thin_pool_metadata_percent > 80
        for: 10m
        labels:
          severity: warning
          triage: none
        annotations:
          summary: '{{ $labels.host }} thin pool {{ $labels.vg }}/{{ $labels.lv }} metadata at {{ $value | printf "%.1f" }}%'
          description: 'Thin-pool METADATA is filling — a different exhaustion from data%, with the same outcome (pool switches to read-only). Grow it on {{ $labels.host }}: `lvextend --poolmetadatasize +1G {{ $labels.vg }}/{{ $labels.lv }}` needs free VG extents, which pve''s VG does not have; plan the fix, do not wait for 100%.'
      - alert: PveVmIoError
        expr: pve_qemu_status{qmpstatus="io-error"} == 1
        for: 1m
        labels:
          severity: critical
          triage: none
        annotations:
          summary: '{{ $labels.host }} guest {{ $labels.name }} (vmid {{ $labels.vmid }}) is paused on io-error'
          description: 'The guest hit a failed write and QEMU paused it — the 2026-09-03 signature (thin pool at 100%). `qm list` still says running; the Talos API is "no route to host". On {{ $labels.host }}: `lvs -o lv_name,data_percent`, free pool space, then `qm resume {{ $labels.vmid }}`.'
      - alert: PveHostMemoryLow
        expr: node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes{job="pve-node"} < 3 * 1024 * 1024 * 1024
        for: 15m
        labels:
          severity: warning
          triage: none
        annotations:
          summary: '{{ $labels.host }} memory available {{ $value | humanize1024 }}B — below 3GiB'
          description: 'A hypervisor is close to committing more VM RAM than it has. pve is the known case (65GB of allocations on 62.7GiB since 2026-09-08, FU-225, living on KSM ~11GB shared); Talos guests have no virtio_balloon, so when KSM sharing collapses or a guest grows, the host swaps and then OOM-kills a VM. On {{ $labels.host }}: `free -m`, `cat /sys/kernel/mm/ksm/pages_sharing`, the per-VM RSS (`ps -o rss= -p $(cat /var/run/qemu-server/<vmid>.pid)`); relief is a smaller allocation in `tofu/variables.tf` (reboots that VM), or the VM moves to the other hypervisor — never a RAM buy for pve (operator direction: the second hypervisor was the answer).'
      - alert: PveHostSwapUsed
        expr: (node_memory_SwapTotal_bytes{job="pve-node"} - node_memory_SwapFree_bytes{job="pve-node"}) > 1 * 1024 * 1024 * 1024
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
          triage: none
        annotations:
          summary: '{{ $labels.host }} has {{ $value | humanize1024 }}B in swap'
          description: 'A hypervisor is paging guest memory out. This is the PRECURSOR with hours of lead: nx-02 crossed 1 GiB at 2026-09-24 ~23:00Z and the CI stalls came at 07:24 next morning when those pages were needed back (FU-289). PveHostMemoryLow stays quiet for it — the box had 9 GiB available globally while kswapd emptied one NUMA node. On {{ $labels.host }}: which guest (`pve_qemu_swap_bytes{host="{{ $labels.host }}"}`), which node is starved (`node_memory_numa_MemFree{host="{{ $labels.host }}"}`), what is pinned there (`pve_qemu_numa_resident_bytes`, `pve_qemu_locked_bytes`). Relief is placement — hugepages + `numaN: hostnodes=` on the pinned guest, a restart — or a smaller allocation; while the swap lives on a spinning disk this becomes PveHostSwapThrash.'
      - alert: PveHostSwapThrash
        expr: rate(node_vmstat_pswpin{job="pve-node"}[5m]) * 4096 > 256 * 1024 and rate(node_pressure_io_waiting_seconds_total{job="pve-node"}[5m]) > 0.05
        for: 5m
        labels:
          severity: critical
          triage: now
        annotations:
          summary: '{{ $labels.host }} swapping in at {{ $value | humanize1024 }}B/s with I/O pressure'
          description: 'Guest memory is coming back from swap at disk speed and the host is I/O-bound on it — every guest on {{ $labels.host }} crawls (a CI runner in this state took 397 s for a 2 s step, FU-289; the 09-25 runs showed ~1 MiB/s swap-in at 20% I/O pressure, i.e. a 5400 rpm disk at 98%). It ends when the pages are back, not when memory frees. Now: stop adding load on {{ $labels.host }} (park the runner: `var.ci_runner_0N_running=false`); then the cause via PveHostSwapUsed. Structural: swap on the SSD/NVMe, never the spinner.'
      - alert: PveNumaNodeMemoryLow
        expr: (node_memory_numa_MemFree{job="pve-node"} + node_memory_numa_Inactive_file{job="pve-node"}) < 1536 * 1024 * 1024
        for: 15m
        labels:
          severity: warning
          triage: none
        annotations:
          summary: '{{ $labels.host }} NUMA node {{ $labels.node }} down to {{ $value | humanize1024 }}B free + reclaimable file'
          description: 'One physical memory node of a dual-socket hypervisor is nearly exhausted while the other may be wide open. MemAvailable hides it, and kswapd works PER NODE: it evicts whatever is evictable on that node — the unpinned guests, to swap — to hold its watermarks, regardless of free RAM on the other node (FU-289: wk-04 pinned 6/26 GiB across the nodes of nx-02, node 1 at ~1 GiB, ci-runner-02 went to the HDD). On {{ $labels.host }}: `pve_qemu_numa_resident_bytes{host="{{ $labels.host }}",node="{{ $labels.node }}"}` names the guests on that node, `pve_qemu_locked_bytes` which is pinned (VFIO). A pinned guest is rebalanced only by a restart with explicit `numaN: hostnodes=` (+ hugepages) — docs/spikes/nx-02-numa-placement.md.'
      - alert: PveGuestSwapped
        expr: pve_qemu_swap_bytes > 512 * 1024 * 1024
        for: 15m
        labels:
          severity: warning
          triage: none
        annotations:
          summary: '{{ $labels.host }} guest {{ $labels.name }} (vmid {{ $labels.vmid }}) has {{ $value | humanize1024 }}B on host swap'
          description: The host has paged part of this guest out. The guest cannot know — it sees its RAM as present — so every touch of a swapped page is a disk read it did not ask for; a Talos node or a CI runner in this state stalls for minutes, and a control-plane guest here is an etcd member on a spinning disk. Find the pressure with PveHostSwapUsed / PveNumaNodeMemoryLow on {{ $labels.host }}; the guest recovers when its pages are back (`vmstat 1` on the host, column si), not when this clears.
      - alert: PveMetricsStale
        expr: time() - node_textfile_mtime_seconds{job="pve-node", file=~".*/pve.prom"} > 600
        for: 5m
        labels:
          severity: warning
          triage: none
        annotations:
          summary: '{{ $labels.host }} textfile metrics not refreshed for {{ $value | humanizeDuration }}'
          description: 'node_exporter on {{ $labels.host }} is up but pve.prom has stopped advancing — the collector timer (pve-textfile-metrics.timer) is failing or stopped, so the pool level above is a stale number, not a calm pool. On {{ $labels.host }}: `systemctl status pve-textfile-metrics.timer pve-textfile-metrics.service`; `journalctl -u pve-textfile-metrics`. Re-run `ansible/pve-node-exporter.yml` to reassert the units.'
      - alert: PveMetricsAbsent
        expr: absent(pve_lvm_thin_pool_data_percent{job="pve-node", host="pve"}) or absent(pve_lvm_thin_pool_data_percent{job="pve-node", host="nx-02"})
        for: 15m
        labels:
          severity: warning
          triage: dig
        annotations:
          summary: '{{ $labels.host }} thin-pool meter absent — that pool is unmetered again'
          description: 'No pve_lvm_thin_pool_data_percent series from {{ $labels.host }} for 15m: node_exporter there is down, the ScrapeConfig lost its target, or the textfile is missing. Every pool alert for that box is blind while this fires, and the runner-image-prepull gate holds pool-VM pulls (fail closed, on the max across BOTH pools). Check `curl <host>:9100/metrics | grep pve_lvm` from the LAN (pve 192.168.2.3, nx-02 192.168.2.59), then the ScrapeConfig (`kubectl -n monitoring get scrapeconfig pve-node`) and Prometheus targets.'
